  "published": "2026-11-26",
  "updated": "2026-11-26",
  "intro": "Fidelity, Charles Schwab, Merrill Edge, E*TRADE, Betterment and Webull are six Vanguard alternatives with published fee schedules for self-directed investing or automated portfolios. Online stock and exchange-traded fund (ETF) trades cost $0 in commissions at Vanguard and at five of these brokerages, while Betterment charges an account-level monthly or annual fee. \n\n[Vanguard](https://investor.vanguard.com) operates under a fee schedule effective July 10, 2026. Fees and commissions at Vanguard depend on Qualifying Assets (QA) held across accounts; as QA rises, fees and commissions generally fall. For investors with QA under $1 million, Vanguard charges $0 commission for online stock and ETF trades, $0 commission plus $1 per contract for options, and $20 online for non-Vanguard transaction-fee (TF) mutual funds. A $25 broker-assisted commission (BAC) applies to phone trades with a Vanguard representative.\n\nAccount service rules and trading terms create dollar differences across these six brokerages. For broader context on account costs, see our guide on [what do brokers charge to trade](/guides/what-do-brokers-charge-to-trade/) and our [best investment apps for beginners roundup](/roundup/best-investment-apps-for-beginners/). Fixed-income investors can review the [best brokers for treasury bonds roundup](/roundup/best-brokers-for-treasury-bonds/), and traders comparing specific firms can explore our [Fidelity alternatives](/alternatives/fidelity/) guide, our [Robinhood alternatives](/alternatives/robinhood/) page, and the broader [comparison hub](/compare/).",
