  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Vanguard Alternative",
    "body": "Start by identifying the assets you trade and the order types you submit. Vanguard charges $0 for online stock and ETF trades, but adds a $1-per-contract fee for options trades on accounts with QA under $1 million. Charles Schwab and E*TRADE charge $0.65 per options contract, while E*TRADE lowers that rate to 50 cents per contract for accounts placing 30 or more trades per quarter. Webull charges $0 broker commissions on standard US exchange-listed equity options, with a $0.50 per-contract fee on certain index options. Trading 100 equity options contracts costs $100 at Vanguard ($1 multiplied by 100), $65 at Charles Schwab ($0.65 multiplied by 100), $65 at E*TRADE standard pricing, $50 at E*TRADE quarterly active pricing, and $0 in broker commissions at Webull.\n\nNext, evaluate mutual fund charges and transaction rules. Vanguard charges $0 online for Vanguard mutual funds, though certain Vanguard funds carry special purchase or redemption fees of 0.25% to 1.00% paid directly to the fund. For non-Vanguard mutual funds, Vanguard charges $0 online for no-transaction-fee (NTF) funds and $20 online for transaction-fee (TF) funds when QA sits below $1 million. Placing a TF mutual fund purchase through a Vanguard representative by phone adds the $25 broker-assisted commission (BAC), bringing the fee to $45 ($20 plus $25). Vanguard also assesses a $50 early redemption fee on non-Vanguard funds sold within 60 calendar days of purchase, and charges $3 per transaction for dollar-cost-averaging programs in TF funds. Fidelity lists non-Fidelity NTF mutual funds at $0 online, and E*TRADE lists $0 online commissions on mutual funds.\n\nAccount-level fees and outbound transfer charges represent a third consideration. Vanguard charges a $25 annual account service fee for accounts with QA under $1 million, but waives it for clients who elect electronic delivery of statements and notifications, for Vanguard Cash Plus accounts, or for organization and trust accounts registered under an employer identification number. Fidelity, Charles Schwab, and Webull list no account opening minimums. When moving assets out of a brokerage, Merrill Edge charges $49.95 for a full account transfer and $0 for a partial account transfer. Webull charges $75 per outgoing stock transfer. Merrill Edge also publishes a $24.95 wire transfer fee.\n\nReview how each firm charges for portfolio management if you want automated rebalancing. Vanguard offers advisory services with fees determined by program terms. Betterment operates on an advisory structure, charging $5 per month on digital investing balances between $0 and $24,000 without recurring deposits, or 0.25% annually once an account maintains $200 or more in monthly recurring deposits or reaches a $24,000 balance. Betterment Premium charges 0.65% annually and includes access to an advisor. Betterment waives wrap fees on its self-directed stock and ETF investing accounts.\n\nBefore picking a Vanguard alternative, check these four operational points:\n\n- How many options contracts do you buy or sell each year, and does the firm charge $1, $0.65, or $0 per contract?\n- Do you invest in non-proprietary mutual funds that trigger Vanguard's $20 online TF charge or $50 early redemption fee?\n- Do you require phone support with a live broker, which adds a $25 fee at Vanguard and Charles Schwab?\n- Will you transfer securities out to another institution, where Webull charges $75 and Merrill Edge charges $49.95 for a full transfer?"
  },
