    {
      "heading": "Charles Schwab Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Charles Schwab provides self-directed brokerage accounts with $0 online equity commissions and no minimum balance requirements to open or maintain an account. Standard options trading costs a $0 base commission plus $0.65 per contract. Broker-assisted trades placed by phone with a Charles Schwab representative add a $25 service charge. For digital assets, Charles Schwab lists cryptocurrency trading at 0.75% of trade value per buy or sell, with a minimum fee of $0.01.\n\nCharles Schwab fits options traders who want a published per-contract rate of $0.65, as well as investors seeking equity trades with no account minimums.\n\nVersus Vanguard, Charles Schwab charges $0.35 less per options contract on standard orders. Vanguard charges a $1-per-contract fee on options trades for accounts with QA under $1 million. Both brokerages charge $25 for broker-assisted phone trades: Charles Schwab applies a $25 service charge, and Vanguard applies a $25 broker-assisted commission (BAC) on phone transactions for QA under $1 million. For cryptocurrency, Charles Schwab publishes an online trading fee of 0.75% of trade value, whereas Vanguard's standard brokerage schedule does not list spot cryptocurrency trading commissions.\n\nWorked cost example: An investor placing 100 options contracts online across a calendar year pays $65 at Charles Schwab ($0.65 multiplied by 100). That same volume costs $100 at Vanguard under its $1-per-contract fee for QA under $1 million, creating a $35 difference. Placing one broker-assisted stock trade costs $25 in representative fees at Charles Schwab ($0 online plus $25 service charge) and $25 at Vanguard ($0 online plus $25 BAC)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Merrill Edge Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Merrill Edge Self-Directed accounts require no minimum initial investment and charge $0 for online stock, ETF, and options trades. The Merrill Edge pricing page notes that options contract and other fees may apply, but does not state the per-contract dollar amount on its primary overview. For account administration, Merrill Edge charges $0 for partial account transfers and $49.95 for a full account transfer. Outgoing wire transfers cost $24.95.\n\nMerrill Edge fits self-directed investors who want $0 online stock commissions, no minimum balance requirements, and free partial account transfers.\n\nVersus Vanguard, Merrill Edge lists fixed fees for outbound transfers and wires on its general investing accounts. A full account transfer out of Merrill Edge costs $49.95, while a wire transfer costs $24.95. Vanguard charges a $25 annual account service fee for accounts with QA under $1 million, which is waived through e-delivery enrollment. Vanguard charges $20 online for non-Vanguard transaction-fee (TF) mutual funds, whereas Merrill Edge does not itemize non-proprietary mutual fund transaction costs on its main pricing summary.\n\nWorked cost example: An investor moving half of an investment portfolio to another institution pays $0 at Merrill Edge for a partial transfer. Moving the entire account out costs $49.95. An investor sending two outgoing wires from Merrill Edge pays $49.90 ($24.95 multiplied by 2). If that investor maintains a Vanguard account without selecting electronic document delivery, Vanguard charges $25 annually for accounts with QA under $1 million."
    },
    {
      "heading": "E*TRADE Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "E*TRADE, part of Morgan Stanley, offers $0 online commissions on stocks, options, mutual funds, and ETFs. Options contracts cost $0.65 each, dropping to 50 cents per contract for investors who execute 30 or more stock, ETF, or options trades per quarter. For futures traders, standard futures contracts cost $1.50 per contract per side plus applicable fees, while E-nano Equity Index futures cost $0.75 per contract per side. Secondary-market bonds cost $1.00 per bond, with a $10 minimum and a $250 maximum. Cryptocurrency trading carries a commission of 0.50% of the trade's dollar value. E*TRADE advertises a new-client bonus offering up to $1,500 for accounts funded by February 28, 2027, which is a time-limited promotion.\n\nE*TRADE fits active options traders who reach 30 trades per quarter to access 50-cent contract pricing, as well as investors trading futures and bonds.\n\nVersus Vanguard, E*TRADE publishes specific options tiers and futures pricing. Vanguard charges $1 per options contract for QA under $1 million and does not publish futures contracts on its core fee schedule. \n\nWorked cost example: A trader executing 200 options contracts in a year at E*TRADE pays $130 at the standard $0.65 rate ($0.65 multiplied by 200), or $100 if qualifying for the 50-cent tier ($0.50 multiplied by 200). At Vanguard, 200 contracts cost $200 ($1 multiplied by 200) for an investor with QA under $1 million, a difference of $70 to $100. Purchasing five secondary-market bonds at E*TRADE hits the $10 minimum fee ($1.00 multiplied by 5 is $5, so the $10 minimum applies)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Betterment Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Betterment provides automated investing portfolios alongside self-directed trading accounts. Its digital investing tier costs $5 per month for account balances between $0 and $24,000. That charge shifts to an annual advisory fee of 0.25% once an account maintains $200 or more in monthly recurring deposits, or when the investing balance reaches $24,000 to $1,000,000. Account balances above $1,000,000 receive progressive fee discounts. Betterment Premium charges a 0.65% annual advisory fee and includes access to an advisor. Betterment requires no minimum account balance, waives wrap fees on self-directed stock and ETF trading, and offers a Cash Reserve account with no account fees or balance minimums at a variable annual percentage yield (APY).\n\nBetterment fits investors who want automated portfolio rebalancing at a percentage rate, or who make recurring monthly deposits of $200 or more.\n\nVersus Vanguard, Betterment operates as an automated investment manager rather than a self-directed brokerage. Vanguard charges $0 in commissions for self-directed online stock and ETF trades, assessing management fees only if clients enroll in separate advisory programs. Betterment charges $5 per month or 0.25% annually on its automated portfolios, but waives wrap fees when clients choose self-directed stock and ETF investing. Vanguard offers individual mutual fund purchases with $0 commissions on Vanguard funds.\n\nWorked cost example: An investor with a $10,000 automated balance at Betterment who does not set up a recurring deposit pays $5 per month, which totals $60 per year ($5 multiplied by 12). If that investor sets up a $200 monthly recurring deposit, the fee drops to 0.25% annually, which costs $25 per year ($10,000 multiplied by 0.0025). On a $30,000 balance, Betterment Digital charges 0.25%, totaling $75 per year ($30,000 multiplied by 0.0025), while Betterment Premium at 0.65% costs $195 per year ($30,000 multiplied by 0.0065). At Vanguard, a self-directed investor managing a $30,000 ETF portfolio online with e-delivery pays $0 in account and commission fees."
    },
