  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Vanguard Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Vanguard remains the closer fit for investors building long-term portfolios around Vanguard mutual funds and ETFs. Vanguard charges $0 to trade Vanguard mutual funds online. Certain Vanguard funds require minimum initial investments of $1,000 or $5,000, while a small number of specialized funds apply purchase or redemption fees of 0.25% to 1.00% paid directly into fund assets. Vanguard accounts with electronic statement delivery waive the $25 annual account service fee, allowing investors to hold self-directed Vanguard mutual funds and ETFs at zero ongoing platform cost.\n\nInvestors with substantial balances benefit from Vanguard's Qualifying Assets (QA) framework. As QA increases, fee reductions take effect. Vanguard offers a limited number of commission-free options and transaction-fee mutual fund trades to eligible clients with higher QA tiers, with allowances resetting each calendar year. Options exercises and assignments remain commission-free across all QA levels. Non-Vanguard no-transaction-fee (NTF) mutual funds trade at $0 online for all account holders.\n\nWho This Is Not For\n\n- Active options traders: Vanguard charges $1 per options contract for QA under $1 million. Charles Schwab and E*TRADE charge $0.65, E*TRADE charges $0.50 at 30 or more trades per quarter, and Webull charges $0 in broker commissions on standard equity options.\n- Frequent non-Vanguard mutual fund buyers: Vanguard charges $20 online for non-Vanguard transaction-fee funds, $45 if broker-assisted, and $50 for early redemptions within 60 days. \n- Investors who place orders through a phone representative: Vanguard applies a $25 broker-assisted commission (BAC) on phone trades for QA under $1 million. Charles Schwab also adds a $25 service charge.\n- Automated robo-investing seekers: Vanguard charges advisory fees for managed accounts rather than offering digital advisory services under self-directed pricing. Betterment provides automated portfolio rebalancing starting at $5 per month or 0.25% annually.\n\nWhat Would Change Our Answer on Vanguard Alternatives\n\n- Vanguard reducing or eliminating its $1-per-contract options fee for accounts with QA under $1 million.\n- Vanguard removing the $20 online transaction fee on third-party mutual funds or eliminating the $50 early redemption fee.\n- Charles Schwab or E*TRADE raising their options contract fees above $0.65 per contract.\n- Betterment altering its $5 monthly charge on balances under $24,000 or changing the $200 monthly recurring deposit requirement.\n- Webull introducing account maintenance fees or increasing index option charges above $0.50 per contract."
  },
